A||N||14||Voytek||A/C CONVERSION: GREAT ALTERNATIVE||||3||
Z||000000||Voytek||07-25-2001||06:39 PM||||Ok.  This is FYI for anyone who is thinking about repairing OR converting their A/C system.<P>I had my system completed today and it blow ICE COLD air.  Here's what I did:<BR>First, the compressor was dead - I replaced it together with the receiver/drier (silver can up front).  This is a must if you don't want the compressor warranty to be invalid.<BR>Now, here's the important part:<BR>I had the system re-filled with Duracool.  I've mentioned this product on here somewhere before.  After I found out how much it costs to convert to R134 and the pressure the system operates at, I decided to look into any alternatives, hence Duracool.  You can read about this stuff on the web.<P>Continuing on, I went to a local authorized shop.  They vacuumed the system and checked if it holds (along with a few other little checks).  They came the re-charge.  This stuff is compatible with all A/C systems (R12 OR R134).  The only thing that needed to be changed is two fittings on the filter/drier.  The re-charge took about 15 minutes and included some additional oil into the receiver/drier (which I forgot to do).  Now the good parts: On max A/C, the system's temperature measured 34 deg F inside the vent.  THIS STUFF IS COLD!  They actually pumped a bit extra refrigerant into the system to raise the temperature (there was frost on the filter/drier!)  Right now I can run it on Normal and feel really cool.<P>The other good part is the operating pressure in the system; (on a cool day) it ran at about 48 psi.  It will run up to about 90 psi on a hot day and prolonged operation.  Compare this with R134, which runs at pressures of around 200 psi!  Benefit?  A lot lower probability of system leakage and extended life.<P>The costs?  I paid $175 CDN (about $110 USD) for the entire operation.  A re-charge costs about $50 bucks CDN (professionally done - you can also buy cans at parts stores).<P>Just something to consider if you're trying to fix your system (or just re-charge it).<P>P.S. You may find information on the web that Duracool is illegal in 18 states.  Apparently that is misleading.  It is UNRECOGNIZED in 18 states but illegal in only 2.  California was one of the first states to recognize Duracool.  There are no restrictions in Canada.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000001||Terrybogin||07-25-2001||08:06 PM||||Is there a new, non-flamable version of Duracool? Or is still a CNG? I tried some of the early r-12 substitutes, for evaluation purposes only, and some are really COLD! I just did not like the ones that were basically butane/propane with a little stench added. It sounds like you have a great system with such a low head pressure, please keep us updated on how it lasts.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Johnny07||07-25-2001||09:26 PM||||just ran a search for a local place to do this and got an EPA site also. unfortunately the result was it's a no-no in autos<P>check link <A HREF="http://www.epa.gov/spdpublc/title6/snap/ref.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.epa.gov/spdpublc/title6/snap/ref.html</A> <BR> see section 4<P>this sort of sucks since it's been 95 out and the r134 just isn't cutting it||||reg||1
Z||000003||Johnny07||07-25-2001||09:29 PM||||sorry wrong link<P>this is the right one<BR> <A HREF="http://www.epa.gov/spdpublc/title6/snap/hc-12a.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.epa.gov/spdpublc/title6/snap/hc-12a.html</A> <P>still section 4||||reg||1
Z||000004||TooManyFieros||07-26-2001||07:06 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Johnny07:<BR>I think the reason it's illegal is the fact that it's flamable. Imagine someone in your car lights up a smoke while the propane slowly leaks into your car. I know it's probably not going to happen but could it?<BR>I believe that if you had used R-134A after doing this work you would have been amazed. It's performance or lack of performance is usually due to how your system is prepared and what shape the compressor and oriface valve are in. Yesterday, here in PA, it was 96 deg and humid. I didn't measure the outlet temp but I had to slow down the fan before I froze in an 88GT 134-A. I have no complaints about 134-A and have it in all my cars.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Voytek||07-26-2001||11:15 AM||||TooManyFieros - no doubt that if your system runs really good, you may get some pretty low temperatures out of R134A.  Most vehicles, however, are NOT very efficient with R134A.<BR>The biggest negative, though, is the cost of conversion from R12 to R134.  The kits that Napa sells ($70 bucks here) are not adequate.  A proper conversion should also see the hoses changed.  This kind of conversion runs between $400 and $500 bucks here.  Duracool conversion costs $175 CDN.<P>Terrybogin - Yes, Duracool is flammable but nowhere near as harmful to your health as R134.  Also, I assume you have a Fiero, right?  Have you ever heard that there is a potential for fire in a Fiero?  So have I.  I've had 5 Fieros - the rumor hasn't phased me yet.  And how's this: California has some pretty stringent automotive requirements, right?  They were one of the first states to recognize this stuff.  I've got one more for you.  Do you realize that you've got a gas tank between the seats in your Fiero?  That's some pretty flammable stuff you're driving around with.<P>I'm not trying to put you down but I hope you understand what I'm saying.  The stuff's been in use around here for over 5 years now and I haven't heard of anyone's car exploding yet (hopefully mine won't be the first  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> )<BR>Oh, by the way, this IS Propane based, but it is not just a mix of Butane and Propane.  It's an engineered product with some other ingredients in it (or so I'm told, anyways).||||reg||1
Z||000006||TylerB11||07-26-2001||11:19 AM||||Incase people are lazy - here is the quote from the EPA:<P>"May hydrocarbon refrigerants be used to replace CFC-12, commonly referred to as "Freon® ," in cars?<BR>No. It is illegal to use hydrocarbon refrigerants like HC-12a® and DURACOOL 12a® as substitutes for CFC-12 in automobile or truck air conditioning under any circumstances."<P>Sorry man, its completely illegal for use in the US.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Voytek||07-26-2001||11:38 AM||||I don't know how old the info is on this website but this is NOT true.  IN FACT, the brochure I got from a local parts store that sells the stuff says the same thing: illegal for any automotive use.  GO FIGURE!  There are shops in town OFFICIALLY installing it!!<BR>I've read on A website (I'm not sure which one but it talked about all the available refrigerants on the market) that it was illegal in 18 states.  Later I found out that even this info wasn't accurate.  18 states don't RECOGNIZE it but only 2 have made it illegal.<BR>On the other hand, your gov't is pretty anal about some things.  This stuff originated in Australia where it's being pretty widely used today AND is now also used in Europe with some countries officially recognizing it as the new environmentally friendly refrigerant.<BR>Think about one more thing: R134 may not be flammable but the system operates at 200 psi and up.  If something were to explode at that pressure near your engine, you MAY have a fire, even if R134 is not flammable.  My system was running at 48 psi yesterday after re-charge.  It was a relatively cool day and I'm told that I MAY see the pressure rise to 90 psi (MAYBE).  As you can see the potential for leakage is a lot lower than with R134 (OR R12).<BR>Nonetheless, don't do anything illegal, ya hear?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Johnny07||07-26-2001||01:30 PM||||not to say I haven't thought about using this stuff but....<BR>this is this update info on the site<P>Written by EPA's Global Programs Division<P><BR>Last updated on May 1, 2001 <A HREF="http://www.epa.gov/ozone/title6/snap/hc-12a.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.epa.gov/ozone/title6/snap/hc-12a.html</A>  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Voytek||07-26-2001||01:59 PM||||Yes, I've read this website before I did the conversion.  It DID scare me at first but then I decided to get the story from the other side.  It's not as bad as it sounds.  I've been talking to the manufacturer direct, to local reps and to shops, as well as further research on the web.  I definitely didn't want to sit on something that could blow my butt to pieces.  Like I said, the government can be pretty anal about some things but once you get all the facts, it's not that bad.<BR>Think about this: I don't think I need to ask you if you've heard of Firestone tires exploding, right?  OK, now, have you heard of car fire / explosion incidents caused by this new refrigerant?  I have not.  Why would many governments throughout the world legalize this product, while few (like the US) are hesitant? <P>In any case, I don't sell the stuff and couldn't care less if anyone else out there uses it.  I am posting my results on here because it's definitely worked for me and maybe it can save some other Fiero owners money.  I think it's a good alternative, even if only in Canada and Australia.<P>Now I think I'll go outside to my Fiero and crank the A/C - it's getting warm in here.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||07-26-2001||08:07 PM||||When it comes to flamable refrigerant, the USEPA doesn't care about the degree of flamability. It's flamable and therefor banned for automotive use. Shops installing it can be in real deep $hit. There are huge fines for shops installing illegal refrigerants. It's not up to the states.<P>A big reason for this is there is no way to reliably test the flamables under all sets of posible install conditions and failures. No manufacturor can test this either. Just because they can't set it off in lab conditions doesn't make it safe.<P>The simple fact is AC parts fail and leak. No refrigerant is good for you. They simply don't want the potential for explosive problems rolling down the highway. (Like cars aren't burning up all the time already...) When you look at how poorly many shops, and worse the DIY market, do AC work, this is a very real concern. A burnt wire or fuel leak usually causes a fire not an explosion, butane/propane/whatever inside the car can create a rather huge bomb should it go off. Such an explosion has the potential to kill any one in range. It could be a considerable range, even effecting housing along the highway.<P>Eventually the rules may change but don't hold your breath waiting. It will continue to be federally illegal for the forseeable future.<P>If you want more info dig around in the various auto repair trade rags. MotorAge.com Motor.com Babcox.com IMcool.com Aircondition.com (Mixed trade DIY with message boards.)<P>To make it more interesting.... Some Flamables are legal in some stationary and truck trailer applications. They figure less likelyhood for ignition.... It's because of the way most of those systems are built plus the fact there is little to no DIY work on them.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Voytek||07-27-2001||12:37 PM||||Well, I, for one, don't think that my whole 1.8 lbs of this refrigerant, which is what my Fiero took (diluted with oil AND other ingredients) poses any risks to me, should it ever explode and DEFINITELY not to any surrounding homes (????????????????!!!!!!!!!)<P>In our city/province it is illegal to do many things, EXCEPT fill your system with hydrocarbon refrigerant that is now officially sold by parts stores.<BR>Shops getting in deep sh$t for filling systems with Duracool?  Well, just so you know, in places where it IS legal, the shop will put (as they did with my car and are REQUIRED by law) a sticker at the receiver/drier WITH THEIR NAME ON IT right next to the type of refrigerant that is in the system.  I think THAT would make it definitely NOT illegal (eg. you wouldn't want to give someone a receipt for selling them a joint, now, would you?).  By the same token, if a shop in your state is willing to put a sticker with their name after a recharge with Hydrocarbon type of refrigerant, the stuff is very likely NOT illegal, just not officially recognized.  Denmark's gov't has officially recognized this refrigerant for automotive use.  I haven't heard of any explosions yet.<BR>On the other hand, maybe you guys in the good ol' US of A HAVE outlawed this kind of refrigerants.  It's possible.  You also still haven't converted to the metric system.<P>One other thing.  Your comment 'just because they can't set it off in lab condions doesn't make it safe' - well, it doesn't make it unsafe, either.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||artherd||07-27-2001||08:56 PM||||1.8bls of LQUIFIED propane is not a small ammount of gas...<P>ALthough to reach explosive concentration, it would have to mix with air at the correct ratio *in a confined space*.<P>That leaves the interior of the car, where the stench would literally make you THORW UP before there was danger of ignition, or inside the system itself (by then there would be very little gas left, as the pressure would be reduced to atmospheric, the lquid all boiled off, AND enough air get in to produce an combustable mix. All that would happen is likely burst a hose. <P>In a collision, I would be much more worried about the 50gallons of essentially NAPALM in the F150 truck that just hit you.  The fiero gas tank will probally not ignite due to it's location.<P>And gasolene will burn (thougn not explode) at nearly any "concentration" as it remains lquid.<P>Please note all the above is conjecture, and that I personally will go to 134a long before I put propane in my air conditioner.  Personal preference.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000013||theogre||07-28-2001||12:24 AM||||Voytek, You've missed a big point. What goes on in Canada and what's legal in the U.S. are two diferant animals. Myself and others clearly stated we were talking about USEPA rules on this. We said nothing about Canada. The simple fact is that Most of the people that read this thread will likely be U.S. drivers.<P>When you figure Propane works in car motors and runs close to the same fuel/air ratio, I imagine it won't take very much propane/butane to reach a flamable mix inside the car. (Sometimes evaporators leak slow... many times they blow the wad in minutes to nothing flat.)<P>I've seen what large fuel air explosions can do first hand. On a somewhat larger scale, I saw what was left after a propane explosion in a mobil home. We found peices 1/2 to 3/4 mile away. It blew out windows a mile away. It picked the whole front wall of the trailer and threw it 150+ feet. That was a thousand pound chunk of wall at least.<P>Shrapnel from a fuel/air explosion in a car interior could easily hurl things hundreds of feet a fatal velocity. If you want some real fun reading, dig up the stuff on FAEs in Military lit. It's amazing how small an amount of propane/butane/etc you need to devastate rather large objects. BTW These explosions don't need a confined space. They only need to create a pocket of flamable mass. This can happen anytime the air is slow moving or stagnant in an area. <P>NAFTA means that the U.S. has to evaluate rules in Canada and Mexico that effect trade. It does not require the U.S. to accept those rules. This is the big issue right now with Mexico's mainly crap trucks being driven into the U.S.. Mexico will license nearly anything that runs.<P>Refrigerant issues are along the same lines. You could probably drive the car down here with no legal problem. However it's likely that it's illegal to sell that car to someone down here because of the refrigerant.<P>There are also issues on how to process left over stuff from defunct systems. Many of these products are blends of materials. What do you do with the stuff. If the system has leaked, can you be 100% sure the remaining material is still good? These questions and others have not been answered in any meaningfull way.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||cadaver||07-29-2001||12:29 AM||||I dont know about all this legal stuff. I do know that it cost me $65 to refill my system with R-12. So all this is pretty moot is you ask me.||||reg||1
